msgid "B<RWF_ATOMIC> (since Linux 6.11)"
msgstr "B<RWF_NOWAIT> (Linux 4.14 以降)"

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/readv.2:307
msgid ""
"Requires that writes to regular files in block-based filesystems be issued "
"with torn-write protection.  Torn-write protection means that for a power or "
"any other hardware failure, all or none of the data from the write will be "
"stored, but never a mix of old and new data.  This flag is meaningful only "
"for B<pwritev2>(), and its effect applies only to the data range written by "
"the system call.  The total write length must be power-of-2 and must be "
"sized in the range [I<stx_atomic_write_unit_min>, "
"I<stx_atomic_write_unit_max>].  The write must be at a naturally-aligned "
"offset within the file with respect to the total write length.  For example, "
"a write of length 32KiB at a file offset of 32KiB is permitted, however a "
"write of length 32KiB at a file offset of 48KiB is not permitted.  The upper "
"limit of I<iovcnt> for B<pwritev2>()  is given by the value in "
"I<stx_atomic_write_segments_max.> Torn-write protection only works with "
"B<O_DIRECT> flag, that is, buffered writes are not supported.  To guarantee "
"consistency from the write between a file's in-core state with the storage "
"device, B<O_SYNC> or B<O_DSYNC> must be specified for B<open>(2).  The same "
"synchronized I/O guarantees as described in B<open>(2)  are provided when "
"these flags or their equivalent flags and system calls are used (e.g., if "
"B<RWF_SYNC> is specified for B<pwritev2>())."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/readv.2:307
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "B<RWF_NOWAIT> (since Linux 4.14)"
msgid "B<RWF_DONTCACHE> (since Linux 6.14)"
msgstr "B<RWF_NOWAIT> (Linux 4.14 以降)"

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/readv.2:319
msgid ""
"Reads or writes to a regular file will prune instantiated page cache content "
"when the operation completes.  This is different than normal buffered I/O, "
"where the data usually remains in cache until such time that it gets "
"reclaimed due to memory pressure.  If ranges of the read or written I/O were "
"already in cache before this read or write, then those ranges will not be "
"pruned at I/O completion time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/readv.2:333
msgid ""
"Additionally, any range dirtied by a write operation with B<RWF_DONTCACHE> "
"set will get kicked off for writeback.  This is similar to calling "
"B<sync_file_range>(2)  with I<SYNC_FILE_RANGE_WRITE> to start writeback on "
"the given range.  B<RWF_DONTCACHE> is a hint, or best effort, where no hard "
"guarantees are given on the state of the page cache once the operation "
"completes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/readv.2:340
msgid ""
"If used on a file system or block device that doesn't support it, it will "
"return -1, and I<errno> will be set to B<EOPNOTSUPP>."
